// PARAM: --analysis int
// The refining composite domain (Sign x Interval x Parity x Congruence,
// Refine_Fixpoint mode -- Int_Refinement.thy/Int_Transfer.thy/Int_Base_DG.thy)
// proves y == 2 from y + 1 == 3 by backward-filtering the guard through all
// four components together, reductively refining each against what the
// others already pin down. Neither Sign nor Interval alone proves this on
// the identical program (both report UNKNOWN, y left at Top/[-inf,+inf]
// respectively -- verified via --analysis sign / --analysis interval on
// this same source): int_dom's cross-component reduction is doing real
// work here, not merely running four domains in parallel. Same guard as
// Exec_Int_DG_Run.thy's dgExI_fixpoint_inspect_y_at_Statement_1, now
// reached through the CLI's production Int_Analysis route instead of a
// direct Isabelle solver-state inspection.
void main() {
  int32 x, y;
  if (y + 1 == 3) {
    x := 1;
    __voblint_check(y == 2) // PROVED
  } else {
    x := 0
  }
}
